In this lesson, we'll look at when a function actually has an inverse. So, picture a function as a little machine: you feed it an input, and it spits out an output. The inverse is a machine that runs that process backwards, taking your output and giving you back the original input. Here's the catch, though. For that reverse machine to work properly, each output has to come from exactly one input. Otherwise, when you try to undo it, you'd have one input leading to lots of possible answers, which isn't allowed in a function. Functions that do this are called one-to-one. So the rule is simple. A function only has an inverse function when it is one-to-one. And there's a really quick visual way to check. It's called the horizontal line test. If you imagine sliding a horizontal line across the graph of your function, that function is one-to-one only if the line never touches the graph in more than one place at the same time. Think about a straight line like two x plus one, that's clearly one-to-one, so it has an inverse. But something like x squared isn't, because lots of inputs, like three and negative three, both give nine. That fails the test. You can sometimes rescue it by restricting the domain, for example only looking at x values that are zero or positive. And one last note, when we write f to the minus one, we mean the inverse function, not one over f, so don't mix those two up. Now let's see it in action.